{
  "id": "al7",
  "title": "Jumps",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "al7-l1",
      "title": "JMP",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Normally the 8080 runs one byte after the other. JMP (303) with an address makes it continue somewhere else.",
        "A jump forward skips code. A jump backward repeats code.",
        "The address works like with LDA: low byte, then high byte."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "076   ; 000: MVI A",
          "001   ;      1",
          "303   ; 002: JMP show",
          "007   ;      address 007 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "076   ; 005: MVI A",
          "143   ;      99",
          "323   ; 007: OUT 377   ← show",
          "377   ;      port 377",
          "166   ; 011: HLT"
        ],
        "output": [
          "LEDS ○○○○○○○● = 1"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "al7-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does JMP do?",
          "options": [
            "Continues the program at another address",
            "Stops the program",
            "Shows A",
            "Loads a byte"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "JuMP."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l1-e2",
          "type": "switches",
          "prompt": "Set the switches to JMP (3 0 3).",
          "answer": 195,
          "explanation": "11 000 011."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l1-e3",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "In the example, why don't the lights show 99?",
          "options": [
            "JMP skips over MVI A 99",
            "99 is too big",
            "OUT shows B",
            "HLT comes first"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The jump goes straight to OUT."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l1-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What do the lights show?",
          "code": [
            "076   ; 000: MVI A",
            "005   ;      5",
            "303   ; 002: JMP out",
            "007   ;      address 007 (low byte)",
            "000   ;      (high byte)",
            "306   ; 005: ADI",
            "006   ;      6",
            "323   ; 007: OUT 377   ← out",
            "377   ;      port 377",
            "166   ; 011: HLT"
          ],
          "options": [
            "5",
            "6",
            "11",
            "0"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The jump skips ADI 6."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l1-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "JMP 007 is in the program as 303, 007, …?",
          "options": [
            "000",
            "007",
            "303",
            "377"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The high byte of address 007 is 000."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l1-e6",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Jump to address 012.",
          "code": [
            "303   ; JMP",
            "___   ; low byte",
            "000   ; high byte"
          ],
          "options": [
            "012",
            "000",
            "303",
            "120"
          ],
          "answer": "012",
          "explanation": "The low byte is the address."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "al7-l2",
      "title": "Counting Addresses",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "To jump, you must know the address of the target. Count the bytes: 1-byte instructions (MOV, ADD, HLT), 2-byte ones (MVI, ADI, OUT), 3-byte ones (JMP, LDA, STA, LXI).",
        "If you insert a byte, every address after it moves, and every jump to it must change. That's why programmers kept careful listings with the address of each byte.",
        "The comments in these lessons show the address (in octal) at the start of each instruction."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "006   ; 000: MVI B",
          "002   ;      2",
          "076   ; 002: MVI A",
          "050   ;      40",
          "303   ; 004: JMP end",
          "010   ;      address 010 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "200   ; 007: ADD B",
          "200   ; 010: ADD B   ← end",
          "323   ; 011: OUT 377",
          "377   ;      port 377",
          "166   ; 013: HLT"
        ],
        "output": [
          "LEDS ○○●○●○●○ = 42"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "al7-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How many bytes does MVI A take?",
          "options": [
            "2",
            "1",
            "3",
            "4"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The opcode and the value."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How many bytes does JMP take?",
          "options": [
            "3",
            "2",
            "1",
            "4"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The opcode and a two-byte address."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l2-e3",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "A program is MVI A (2 bytes), ADI (2 bytes), then JMP. At which address is JMP?",
          "options": [
            "004",
            "002",
            "003",
            "005"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "2 + 2 = 4 bytes before it: addresses 000 to 003."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l2-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What do the lights show?",
          "code": [
            "006   ; 000: MVI B",
            "002   ;      2",
            "076   ; 002: MVI A",
            "050   ;      40",
            "303   ; 004: JMP end",
            "010   ;      address 010 (low byte)",
            "000   ;      (high byte)",
            "200   ; 007: ADD B",
            "200   ; 010: ADD B   ← end",
            "323   ; 011: OUT 377",
            "377   ;      port 377",
            "166   ; 013: HLT"
          ],
          "options": [
            "42",
            "44",
            "40",
            "2"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The jump skips the first ADD B, so only one is done: 40 + 2."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l2-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why did old listings show the address of every byte?",
          "options": [
            "To work out the jump targets",
            "To make them longer",
            "The Altair printed them",
            "No reason"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Jumps use exact addresses."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l2-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "You insert one extra byte before a jump target. What must you do?",
          "options": [
            "Add 1 to the target address in every jump to it",
            "Nothing",
            "Remove HLT",
            "Start again at 000"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Everything behind the new byte moved by one."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "al7-l3",
      "title": "Endless Loops",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "A jump back to an earlier address repeats that part forever. On the real Altair, that's how a program keeps running, like a clock or a light show.",
        "In this app, a program must end with HLT. One that never stops is halted after a while: \"The program never reached HLT\".",
        "The next units show how to leave a loop: with a decision."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "076   ; 000: MVI A",
          "001   ;      1",
          "323   ; 002: OUT 377",
          "377   ;      port 377",
          "207   ; 004: ADD A",
          "323   ; 005: OUT 377",
          "377   ;      port 377",
          "207   ; 007: ADD A",
          "323   ; 010: OUT 377",
          "377   ;      port 377",
          "166   ; 012: HLT"
        ],
        "output": [
          "LEDS ○○○○○○○● = 1",
          "LEDS ○○○○○○●○ = 2",
          "LEDS ○○○○○●○○ = 4"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "al7-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does a JMP back to an earlier address do?",
          "options": [
            "Repeats that part of the program",
            "Stops the program",
            "Skips code",
            "Nothing"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Backwards means: again."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What happens to a program that never reaches HLT in this app?",
          "options": [
            "It's stopped with \"The program never reached HLT\"",
            "It runs forever",
            "The phone crashes",
            "It reaches HLT anyway"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "There's a limit on the number of steps."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l3-e3",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program never stops. Which byte makes it loop forever?",
          "code": [
            "076   ; 000: MVI A",
            "001   ;      1",
            "074   ; 002: INR A   ← again",
            "303   ; 003: JMP again",
            "002   ;      address 002",
            "000   ;      (high byte)",
            "166   ; 006: HLT"
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "JMP 002 always goes back, so HLT is never reached."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l3-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How do you leave a loop?",
          "options": [
            "With a conditional jump that sometimes doesn't jump",
            "With JMP",
            "With OUT",
            "You can't"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Next unit: JZ, JNZ, JC and JNC."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l3-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Where were endless loops normal?",
          "options": [
            "In programs that run all the time, like a clock or a game",
            "Nowhere",
            "Only in BASIC",
            "Only on the PDP-11"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "On a real Altair, the operator stopped them with the STOP switch."
        },
        {
          "id": "al7-l3-e6",
          "type": "switches",
          "prompt": "Set the switches to INR A.",
          "answer": 60,
          "explanation": "00 111 100."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
